  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 AM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nResidual low-level moisture early this morning has allowed \nshallow fog to develop across portions of the area, including \ninterior northern Illinois and into portions of northwest \nIndiana. The lowest visibilities are focused mainly where the \ncoverage of high clouds extending north into the area from \nconvection in southwest Illinois is lowest. Generally expect at \nleast patchy fog to persist through daybreak, some of which \ncould be locally dense near low-lying areas and open fields.\n\nThe surface cold front that initiated convection last evening \nhas since washed out over the region. Instead of a true frontal\npassage, the surface flow may simply flop northerly after \ndaybreak this morning for most of the area. Surface flow may \nremain southerly across central IL/IN, however, which will \nlikely set up a corridor of surface convergence near and south \nof a Pontiac to Rensselaer line. This may lead to at least \nisolated shower and thunderstorm development this afternoon \ndepending on the strength of the low-level convergence amidst a \nlargely uncapped environment. Additionally, a reinforcing lake \nbreeze boundary is expected to drift inland today which could \nserve as another lifting mechanism for at least a few showers \ninto portions of the Chicago metro. Also can't fully rule out \nlightning with any of the deeper showers north of the \nKankakee/Illinois Rivers.\n\nMeanwhile a mid-level wave and associated vort currently over \nsouthern South Dakota early this morning is forecast to drift \neast southeast over the area today. This feature may bring the \nnecessary forcing to increase coverage of showers and \nthunderstorms along the aforementioned surface convergence axis.\nIncreasing PWATs ahead of this wave will support rainfall rates\nin excess of 1-2 inches per hour with the heaviest \nshowers/storms. Hi-resolution guidance has remained fairly \nconsistent in developing this corridor of heavy showers and \nthunderstorms over central Illinois and Indiana, including \nportions of the local area, but have wobbled north or south by a\ncounty or two. Due to the increased susceptibility to flooding \nover much of our area, have elected to hoist a Flood Watch for \nLivingston, Ford, Iroquois, and Benton Counties this afternoon \nand evening where confidence is higher in heavy rainfall \noccurring.\n\nSurface high pressure builds into the area on Thursday bringing\na much needed break from the shower and thunderstorm activity. \nLower dewpoints paired with temperatures in the mid 70s to \naround 80 will make for a pleasant day compared the the more \nhumid airmasses we have been experiencing the past couple of \nweeks.\n\nHigh temperatures through the end of the week will remain near \nto slightly below normal, in the mid 70s to lower 80s. \nAdditional waves moving within the upper flow Friday into the \nupcoming weekend may bring additional showers and storms to the \narea at times (30% chance). Dry conditions are then favored into\nearly next week as upper ridging builds into the region.\n\nPetr",
